      "content": "Forgive me, I got a bit carried away with the goblin references. Let's focus on saving your tomato plants.\n\nTo check the pH level of your soil, you can use a soil testing kit or send a sample to a lab for analysis. The ideal pH range for tomatoes is between 6.0 and 6.8. If your soil is too acidic or too alkaline, it can affect nutrient availability and plant growth.\n\nAs for fixing the nitrogen or magnesium issue, you can try adding a balanced fertilizer that contains nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium (NPK). A general-purpose fertilizer like 10-10-10 or 20-20-20 should provide a good starting point. However, if you suspect a specific nutrient deficiency, you can try adding a fertilizer that targets that particular nutrient.\n\nFor example, if you think your plants are lacking magnesium, you can try adding a magnesium-rich fertilizer like Epsom salt (magnesium sulfate) or magnesium sulfate crystals. Just be sure to follow the instructions on the label and avoid over-fertilizing, as this can cause more harm than good.\n\nAs for the goblin in the compost, I was just making a point that even in a seemingly innocuous pile",
